  "strategy": "We start from the normalized parent sequence and explore various optimization techniques to minimize the score. The core approach involves iterative improvement using geometric mean updates, coordinate descent with golden section search, and length exploration. \n\n1. Coordinate Descent Algorithm \n   Perform coordinate descent with golden section search to adjust pairs of coefficients. For each update, find the optimal transfer amount by solving a linear minimization problem using scipy.linalg.solve_triangular. This ensures that the full convolution constraints are satisfied during optimization.\n   \n   Steps:\n   - Normalize the parent sequence to sum=1\n   - Select coordinate pairs based on convolution peaks and random selection\n   - For each pair, perform golden section search for the transfer amount\n   - Maintain the best candidate after each update\n   \n2. Length Exploration with Natural Cubic Spline \n   - Use uniform interpolation to target new lengths as a power of two\n   - Optimize the natural cubic spline basis coefficients with coordinate descent\n   \n3. Objective Function Components \n   We implement the objective function `obj_l1` and its derivatives:\n     *score = 2.0 * n * max_conv / (sum_norm^2)*//convolve_basis where:\n     - `n` is the basis size\n     - `max_conv` is the maximum value of the full convolution\n     - `sum_norm` is the sum of normalized basis elements (at least 0.01)\n\n4. Default Parameters \n   - Basis length: target powers of two (64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192) \n   - Initial budget: 1600 seconds for the core algorithm\n   - Tolerance: 0.1% consecutive improvement triggers a golden section segment refinement\n   \n5. High-level Structure for `propose_candidate` \n   ```python\n   def propose_candidate(parent=None, seed=42, budget=1600):\n       # Initialize the candidate sequence\n       # Add time checks and security validations\n       # Run primary coordinate descent algorithm\n       # If no significant improvement after 6 updates, trigger fallback analysis\n       # Return the best candidate sequence\n       ```\n\n6. Fallback Search Procedure \n   - Switch to a resampling step (natural cubic spline) for new lengths\n   - If fallback fails, revert to the original parent sequence\n   \nComplexity Details:\nThe algorithm searches over basis lengths O(l^#{3}) and coordinates O(c^) per basis.\nThe gradient descent is optimized by directly computing gradients using scipy's sparse diagonal features, making the O(n log n) convolution FFT-able.",
